  clock: 20  # d9e7
  nonce: d9e7  # d9e7
  message: "RECORD-CORRECTION (2026-07-14, dev @ 699239d969) — the 'build a GLOBAL module_to_file_id' framing is CODE-FALSIFIED; residual re-measured; Category-B fix mechanism corrected.\n\n(1) STALE PREMISE. An impact assessment framed the cross-package residual as needing a GLOBAL module_to_file_id. VERIFIED STALE: analyze_python(repo_root) runs ONCE for the whole repo (py.py:5273); _detect_source_roots finds EVERY package src/ in a monorepo layout (py.py:1814-1872); module_to_file_id is built from ALL file_analyses across ALL packages (py.py:5405-5413). The map is ALREADY global, keyed by full source-root-relative dotted module name. >>> This SUPERSEDES WI-tanot's 2026-07-13 note asserting 'analyze() builds module_to_file_id PER-PACKAGE ... needs a GLOBAL cross-package module map (architectural)' — that claim is code-falsified; do not act on it.\n\n(2) FRESH RESIDUAL (self-analysis): 16 workspace-prefix external_symbol phantoms, ALL in/reached-from TEST files:\n  - 8 HUMAN-RULED CARVE-OUT (per the 2026-07-08 ruling; statically invisible): 5 scip_pb2.* protobuf message classes (synthesized at import by _builder.BuildTopDescriptorsAndMessages) + 3 package dunders (hypergumbo_core.__path__/__file__, hypergumbo_core.linkers.__path__). Dominant by edge count (~89 inbound).\n  - 2 INV-jukok test artifacts (zero inbound; ADR-0037 ruling-2 assigns the dangling residue to the sibling endpoint-closure owner).\n  - 6 GENUINELY RESOLVABLE across 3 DISTINCT mechanisms (~1-5 inbound each): Category B (2: rust._analyzer / prisma._analyzer — module_attr_ref to in-tree module-level _analyzer variables); Category D (3: shutil / copy / datetime — stdlib re-exports mislabeled with a WORKSPACE prefix; a VERIFIED double violation: ADR-0041 §3 ecosystem, since is_stdlib_module is fed the workspace-prefixed head and returns False, so ecosystem=stdlib is unreachable; AND ADR-0037 dst_ref identity, the external module_path points into the workspace); Category E (1: bash:hypergumbo CLI invocation).\n\n(3) CATEGORY-B FIX MECHANISM CORRECTED. The 'fix = scope the retarget to the module' assumption is ALSO wrong: the module_attr_ref retarget (py.py:3613) is ALREADY module-scoped — global_symbols.get((real_module, attr)) — and the _analyzer variable IS registered under (module, name) (py.py:5335). So the phantom is NOT a missing-module-scope bug. Its causal chain is tangled and not-yet-fully-traced: from-imports go to symbol_imports NOT module_imports (py.py:1963-1970), only ast.Import populates module_imports (1972-1977) [so there is an unlocated augmentation step, since the module_attr_ref requires module_imports at 3597], AND _collect_local_bindings adds every from-import alias to the INV-fahub shadow set (3408-3410) which the retarget's shadow guard (3612) then suppresses — treating a method-local 'from pkg import submodule as Z' (Z is a MODULE) as a value-shadow. This is a delicate refinement in taint-adjacent resolution code (INV-fahub: a wrong RESOLVED references edge is WORSE than a phantom) for 2 TEST-file phantoms — NOT the low-cost 'module-scoping' win the analysis assumed. Scoped as a distinct follow-up, deliberately NOT bundled.\n\n(4) DISPOSITION. Per the discipline (verify premise, don't grind a stale/shaky-premise test-file long-tail), and a 4-lens ADR/spec/spirit/value analysis (docs/ + playbooks), the human directed: land NO code here beyond correcting the record; the Category-B fix is captured as a scoped follow-up. The 2026-07-08 option-(i) ruling (fix B/D/E then re-scope+close) was premised on cross-package B needing an architectural global map — now false — so the fix-vs-rescope calculus for the 6 low-blast-radius test-file nodes (B delicate/INV-fahub, D a real but tiny-value ADR-0041 §3 relabel, E human-gated bridge-or-9th-carve-out) should be re-confirmed with the corrected premise before executing. INV-nuzas STAYS violated, honestly documented."  # d9e7
